::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> written as a decimal? {
=0.5#Correct. One half of 1 is 0.5.
~0.2#This uses the denominator as the decimal digit. A fraction means divide the numerator by the denominator.
~1.2#This places the numerator and denominator beside each other. Divide 1 by 2 instead.
~2#This uses only the denominator. The fraction represents 1 divided by 2.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> written as a percent? {
~4%#This uses the denominator as the percent. First convert the fraction to a decimal.
~14%#This combines the numerator and denominator as digits. A fraction represents division.
=25%#Correct. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = 0.25, and 0.25 = 25%.
~75%#This represents <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, not <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 0.75 written as a percent? {
~7.5%#This moves the decimal only one place. Multiply by 100 to convert a decimal to a percent.
=75%#Correct. 0.75 × 100 = 75, so 0.75 = 75%.
~0.75%#This keeps the decimal unchanged. Percent form compares the value to 100.
~750%#This moves the decimal one place too far. 0.75 is less than 1, so it is 75%, not 750%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 45% written as a decimal? {
~4.5#This moves the decimal one place. Divide 45 by 100 to convert from percent to decimal.
=0.45#Correct. 45% = <sup>45</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = 0.45.
~45#This keeps the percent number unchanged. A percent must be divided by 100 to become a decimal.
~0.045#This moves the decimal three places. For percent to decimal, move two places left.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> written as a decimal? {
~0.35#This writes the numerator and denominator as decimal digits. Divide 3 by 5.
=0.6#Correct. 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6.
~3.5#This places the numerator and denominator beside each other. A fraction represents division.
~0.15#This multiplies 3 and 5, then writes a decimal. Convert by division instead.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 20% of 45? {
~20#This uses the percent number as the answer. Convert 20% to 0.20 and multiply by 45.
=9#Correct. 20% = 0.20, and 0.20 × 45 = 9.
~25#This adds 20 and 45 in a partial way instead of finding a percent of the number.
~90#This multiplies by 2 instead of 0.20. Percent must be converted to a decimal first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 10% of 80? {
=8#Correct. 10% means one-tenth, and one-tenth of 80 is 8.
~10#This uses the percent number as the answer instead of taking 10% of 80.
~70#This subtracts 10 from 80. Percent problems require multiplication by the percent as a decimal.
~800#This multiplies by 10 instead of 0.10. Convert 10% to 0.10 first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 30% of 50? {
~10#This may come from confusing 30% with one-fifth. Convert 30% to 0.30.
~30#This uses the percent number as the answer. The question asks for 30% of 50.
=15#Correct. 30% = 0.30, and 0.30 × 50 = 15.
~150#This multiplies by 3 instead of 0.30. Percent must be divided by 100 first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student answers 12 out of 20 questions correctly. What percent is correct? {
~12%#This uses the number correct as the percent without comparing it to the total.
~20%#This uses the total as the percent. Percent correct is part divided by whole.
=60%#Correct. 12 ÷ 20 = 0.6, and 0.6 = 60%.
~120%#This compares 20 to 12 instead of 12 to 20. The correct percent must be less than 100%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student answers 18 out of 20 questions correctly. What percent is correct? {
~18%#This uses the number correct as the percent. Divide 18 by 20 first.
=90%#Correct. 18 ÷ 20 = 0.9, and 0.9 = 90%.
~20%#This uses the total as the percent. Percent correct compares the part to the whole.
~110%#This adds the missing 10% incorrectly. Since 18 is less than 20, the percent must be less than 100%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A car travels 90 miles in 3 hours. What is the unit rate? {
~93 miles per hour#This adds miles and hours. A unit rate uses division.
=30 miles per hour#Correct. 90 ÷ 3 = 30, so the car travels 30 miles per hour.
~87 miles per hour#This subtracts hours from miles. Divide miles by hours to find miles per hour.
~270 miles per hour#This multiplies miles by hours. Unit rates compare to 1 unit by dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Six tickets cost $48. What is the cost per ticket? {
~$6#This uses the number of tickets as the cost. Divide the total cost by the number of tickets.
=$8#Correct. $48 ÷ 6 = $8 per ticket.
~$42#This subtracts tickets from cost. A unit rate uses division.
~$288#This multiplies cost by tickets. Per ticket means cost for 1 ticket.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the ratio 6 red tiles to 9 blue tiles written in simplest form? {
~6:9#This is the original ratio, not the simplest form. Divide both parts by their greatest common factor.
=2:3#Correct. Both 6 and 9 divide by 3, so 6:9 simplifies to 2:3.
~3:2#This reverses the order. The ratio is red to blue, so red must remain first.
~15:3#This adds the parts instead of simplifying the ratio.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which fraction is equivalent to 25%? {
~<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#This is 50%, not 25%. Convert 25% to <sup>25</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub>.
=<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#Correct. 25% = <sup>25</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>.
~<sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#This is 75%, not 25%.
~<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>#This uses 25 as the denominator, but 25% means 25 out of 100.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 0.08 written as a percent? {
=8%#Correct. 0.08 × 100 = 8, so 0.08 = 8%.
~0.8%#This moves the decimal one place instead of two. Multiply by 100.
~80%#This moves the decimal three places or treats 0.08 as 0.8. 0.08 is eight hundredths.
~800%#This moves the decimal too far. A decimal less than 1 can be less than 100%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 6% written as a decimal? {
~0.6#This is 60%, not 6%. Move the decimal two places left.
=0.06#Correct. 6% = <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = 0.06.
~6#This keeps the percent number unchanged. Divide by 100 to convert to a decimal.
~0.006#This moves the decimal three places. Percent to decimal requires two places.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says 6% = 0.6. Which correction is best? {
~The student is correct because 6 becomes 0.6.#This treats percent conversion as moving one decimal place. Percent means out of 100.
=6% = 0.06 because 6% means 6 out of 100.#Correct. 6% = <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub> = 0.06.
~6% = 6 because the percent symbol can be removed.#This ignores the meaning of percent. Percent values must be converted by dividing by 100.
~6% = 0.006 because the decimal moves three places.#This moves the decimal too far. Move two places left.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student reads 40 pages in 5 hours. What is the correct unit rate? {
~35 pages per hour#This subtracts 5 from 40. Unit rate requires division.
=8 pages per hour#Correct. 40 ÷ 5 = 8 pages per hour.
~45 pages per hour#This adds pages and hours. A rate compares pages to 1 hour.
~200 pages per hour#This multiplies pages by hours instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> written as a percent? {
~25%#This uses the numerator and denominator as digits. Convert the fraction by dividing.
=40%#Correct. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> = 0.4, and 0.4 = 40%.
~20%#This uses the numerator as the percent. The denominator also matters.
~250%#This places the numerator and denominator together. The value of <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> is less than 1, so the percent is less than 100%.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 0.125 written as a percent? {
~1.25%#This moves the decimal one place instead of two. Multiply by 100.
=12.5%#Correct. 0.125 × 100 = 12.5, so 0.125 = 12.5%.
~125%#This moves the decimal three places. 0.125 is less than 1, so the percent is less than 100%.
~0.125%#This keeps the decimal unchanged. Percent form compares to 100.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 1.2 written as a percent? {
~12%#This moves the decimal one place. Decimal to percent requires multiplying by 100.
=120%#Correct. 1.2 × 100 = 120, so 1.2 = 120%.
~1.2%#This keeps the decimal unchanged. Percent form is out of 100.
~0.12%#This divides by 10 instead of multiplying by 100.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 50% of 38? {
=19#Correct. 50% means one-half, and half of 38 is 19.
~38#This uses the whole amount. 50% means half of the amount.
~50#This uses the percent number as the answer. The question asks for 50% of 38.
~88#This adds 50 and 38 instead of finding half of 38.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is 5% of 60? {
~5#This uses the percent number as the answer. Convert 5% to 0.05 and multiply by 60.
=3#Correct. 5% = 0.05, and 0.05 × 60 = 3.
~30#This treats 5% like 50%. Five percent is much smaller than half.
~300#This multiplies by 5 instead of 0.05. Convert the percent to a decimal first.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student earns 24 points out of 30. What percent did the student earn? {
~24%#This uses the points earned as the percent. Divide points earned by total points.
~30%#This uses the total as the percent. Percent compares part to whole.
=80%#Correct. 24 ÷ 30 = 0.8, and 0.8 = 80%.
~125%#This compares the total to the part instead of the part to the total.
}

::ALG1_U01_L03_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A cyclist travels 150 miles in 3 hours. What is the unit rate? {
~153 miles per hour#This adds miles and hours. A unit rate uses division.
=50 miles per hour#Correct. 150 ÷ 3 = 50 miles per hour.
~147 miles per hour#This subtracts hours from miles. Divide miles by hours.
~450 miles per hour#This multiplies miles by hours. Per hour means for 1 hour, so divide by 3.
}
